There's a single bug in this snippet, and it's that the function
doesn't handle the case where `b` is zero. JavaScript's `/` operator
doesn't throw on division by zero — it returns `Infinity`,
`-Infinity`, or `NaN` (the latter when both operands are zero),
which can silently propagate through the rest of your code and
produce confusing downstream failures.

Suggested fix:

```javascript
function divide(a, b) {
  if (b === 0) throw new RangeError('Division by zero');
  return a / b;
}
```
